MONOLITHIC: SINGLE APP -- > SINGLE SERVER -- > SINGLE DATABASE
MICRO SERVICES: SINGLE APP -- > MULTIPLE SERVERS -- > MULTIPLE DATABASES


 Cost:
- Microservices: Initially HIGHER due to multiple servers, but can be optimised with containers
- Monolithic: Initially LOWER, but scaling can get expensive

 Maintenance:
- Microservices: Complex (monitoring, deployment, inter-service communication)
- Monolithic: Simpler initially, becomes complex as the app grows

 Limitations of Monolithic - Expanded:

1. SERVER PERFORMANCE ✓
   - Single point of failure
   - Cannot scale individual components

2. SCALING ISSUES
   - Must scale entire app even if only one feature is popular
   - Can't optimise resources per service

3. TECHNOLOGY LOCK-IN
   - Entire app uses the same tech stack
   - Hard to adopt new technologies gradually

4. SLOW DEVELOPMENT
   - Large codebase = longer build/test times
   - One bug can bring down the entire application

5. DEPLOYMENT CHALLENGES
   - Small change requires redeploying the entire application
   - Riskier deployments

 Microservices Challenges:
- Network complexity (services talking to each other)
- Data consistency (distributed transactions)
- Debugging difficulty (multiple services, multiple logs)
- Operational overhead (requires DevOps expertise)

 Modern Approach:
Containers (Docker) help solve microservices challenges:
- Each service in its own container
- Easier deployment and scaling
- Consistent environments
- Resource isolation


